WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.300
A common complaint among school voucher
and charter school advocates is that

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:14.230
media coverage of school choice research
is biased. Do they have a point? Two

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.460
colleagues and I examined this by
identifying five pairs of nearly

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.929
identical studies. Each pair measured the
same outcomes and used the same

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:26.650
methodology. The only difference in each
case was the results:

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.880
One study in each pair found a positive
result for school choice, the other a

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.260
negative one. We then examined major
media coverage, tallying national news

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.090
stories and editorials citing each study.
We found that news coverage played no

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.550
favorites: Coverage of positive and
negative studies was evenly balanced.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.180
Editorial pages were another story,
however. They mentioned negative studies

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.219
twice as often as otherwise identical
positive ones, and research was much more

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:58.539
likely to get mentioned by editorials
than by news coverage. Given this

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:03.370
editorial slant, it's easy to see why
media coverage can feel tilted. But news

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.480
coverage played it straight in this case,
and that's a good thing.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:14.160
Do you think media coverage of school choice is fair?
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.360
know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.530
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

